\chapter{Control Plane Structure}

There should be something more detailed to say here, but this is as much
as has been written thus far.

The control plane is complicated by adaptations to cover 4 domains:
\section{Within multikernel\index{multikernel}
		\footcite{baum09} domain framekernel\index{framekernel}
		\footcite{peng24} TCB shared address space direct
		continuation call}
% \section{Within Framekernel Domains: Continuations and State Machines}

Rust appears to have a way to support continuation-passing style.
Within a framekernel domain, state machines and continuations are the
fundamental control flow dispatch mechanism.

		Microkernel minimality limits the scope of applicability
		here, which may be for the best, because manually
		managing continuation state is burdensome.

\section{Kernel-user FlexSC-like\index{FlexSC}\footcite{soar10}
		mapped ring}
% \section{Within Multikernel Domains: Network Protocol Like Message Passing}

Between the multikernel's partitions or subdomains, microkernel-style
message passing is handled in an explicitly network protocol handling
-oriented fashion.
		Most things dealt with by conventional monolithic
		kernels are done via ``userspace'' processes, which then
		involve the syscall API/ABI. The scare quotes are
		because these typically involve highly elevated
		privileges in order to manipulate pagetables
		\index{pagetable} and execute privileged instructions to
		do things like fill the TLB\index{TLB}.

\section{Between multikernel\index{multikernel} domain zero copy
		network-like IPC}
% \section{Within Cluster Nodes: Zero Copy IPC}

		Given that these are meant to align with NUMA
		\index{NUMA} nodes or small, topologically nearby groups
		thereof, a ``remote'' access penalty might be taken, but
		of vastly smaller magnitude than for remote cluster
		\index{cluster} nodes.

\section{Between Cluster\index{clustering} Nodes: Network-Transparent IPC}

		While it's possible to take advantage of shared memory
		that's otherwise largely been partitioned to, say, do
		zero copy data transfer between multikernel
		\index{multikernel} domains typically aligned with
		NUMA\index{NUMA} nodes, cluster\index{clustering} nodes
		are another matter entirely and there's no way to avoid
		having to do much slower sorts of IO operations to
		transmit the data between the nodes.
